{"meta": {"chapter_title": "Foundations of CBI and Design Tensions", "title": "Design Tensions Before Tool Choice", "description": "CAT 531 textbook section connecting design tensions before tool choice to Design Tension Studio map naming a classroom value conflict and negotiated decision, reader notes, support moments, and revision evidence.", "course": "cat531-supplement", "chapter": "01", "section": "02", "learning_objectives": ["Analyze how the Design Tension Studio map naming a classroom value conflict and negotiated decision shows a defensible decision in CAT 531.", "Distinguish artifact polish from evidence-based revision.", "Document a bounded AI/software support move with accepted and rejected suggestions.", "Use annotations, support requests, and revision notes to make a classroom technology decision more defensible."], "concept_ids": ["cat531_supplement_foundations_of_cbi_and_design_tensions", "cat531_supplement_design_tensions_before_tool_choice", "evidence_based_revision", "ai_supported_learning"], "prereq_section_ids": ["cat531-supplement/01/01"], "practice_ids": ["cat531_supplement_1_2_design_tensions_before_tool_choice_artifact_signal", "cat531_supplement_1_2_design_tensions_before_tool_choice_annotation_signal", "cat531_supplement_1_2_design_tensions_before_tool_choice_ai_boundary", "cat531_supplement_1_2_design_tensions_before_tool_choice_calibration"], "estimated_time_minutes": 14, "order": 2, "top_tier_hardened": false}, "content": "# Design Tensions Before Tool Choice\n\n![Design Tensions Before Tool Choice textbook visual](/course-art/deep-exemplars/cat531-supplement/01-02-design-tensions-before-tool-choice.png)\n\n<youtube-embed id=\"yMQiHJsePOM\" title=\"What Is The [TPACK](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Technological_pedagogical_content_knowledge) Model?\" caption=\"Watch after the first read. Use [TPACK](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Technological_pedagogical_content_knowledge) to test whether your technology choice fits the content, pedagogy, and classroom context together.\"></youtube-embed>\n\n## Why This Section Matters\n\nMost bad edtech decisions are not made at the moment of purchase. They are made earlier, when nobody wrote down what the classroom actually wanted and what it was unwilling to give up. By the time a tool is on the table, the conversation has already narrowed to \"does this product do X?\" and the deeper question, \"are we trading away something we care about to get X?\", never gets asked aloud. Naming the design tension before the tool reverses that order on purpose.\n\nA design tension is a genuine conflict between two things you legitimately want, where getting more of one tends to cost you the other. Visible participation versus equitable participation. Fast feedback versus fair feedback. Standardization versus responsiveness. Tensions cannot be \"solved,\" only negotiated, and the negotiation is the teacher's job. This section matters because a tool chosen before the tension is named will silently resolve the tension for you, usually in favor of whatever the vendor optimized for.\n\n## Learning Targets\n\n- Analyze how the Design Tension Studio map naming a classroom value conflict and negotiated decision shows a defensible decision in CAT 531.\n- Distinguish artifact polish from evidence-based revision.\n- Document a bounded AI/software support move with accepted and rejected suggestions.\n- Use annotations, support requests, and revision notes to make a classroom technology decision more defensible.\n\n## Opening Case\n\nA middle-school team wants to lift participation and is about to roll out a gamified-points platform because \"it is fun and other schools use it.\" Notice what has already happened: the tool arrived before anyone said what kind of participation they wanted or what they refused to sacrifice for it. The platform's default is a public leaderboard, which means the team is about to adopt a specific answer (\"make participation a public ranking\") to a question they never debated.\n\nThe record that matters is not that a tool was chosen. It is whether the team named the tension (visible participation versus equity for slower and multilingual learners), set a test the tool had to pass, and showed the trade they accepted.\n\n## Core Concept\n\nA design tension is a structural conflict between two defensible classroom values such that maximizing one degrades the other. It is different from a problem, which has a solution, and different from a preference, which has no cost. The discipline of this section is to identify the tension first and only then judge a tool against it.\n\nWorking in this order produces three things a tool-first process cannot:\n\n- **A named conflict.** \"We want participation visible (A) without turning the room into a ranking that penalizes students who need more processing time (B).\" Stating both poles honestly is the move; a tension with only one pole written down is a slogan.\n- **A pass/fail test the tool must meet.** The test comes from the tension, not the feature list. Here: any candidate must let participation be credited privately or by effort, not only by a public leaderboard.\n- **A visible trade.** Whatever you give up to resolve the tension is stated, so a colleague can challenge the trade rather than the tool.\n\n[TPACK](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Technological_pedagogical_content_knowledge) is the lens for the first pole (does this fit my content and pedagogy?), and critical edtech studies is the lens for the second (who carries the cost this design creates?). When the two lenses pull in different directions, you have found the tension; that pull is the signal, not a problem to smooth over.\n\n<inline-check question=\"A team writes only: 'We want participation to be visible and motivating.' Why is this not yet a design tension?\" explanation=\"A tension needs both poles written honestly. This statement names only Value A (visible participation) with no second pole and no cost, so it is a slogan or a wish, not a negotiation. The missing pole here is equity for slower and multilingual learners.\" options='[{\"text\":\"It names only one pole, so it is a slogan, not a tension\",\"correct\":true},{\"text\":\"It is a tension because participation always conflicts with itself\",\"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"It is a problem that has a single solution\",\"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"It is fine as written once a tool is chosen\",\"correct\":false}]'></inline-check>\n\n## Worked Example\n\nScenario: the middle-school team from the opening case decides what to do about the gamified-points platform. The principle is to map the tension before, not after, the tool. Walk it in four numbered moves.\n\n**Before (tool-first framing).** The team's draft proposal reads:\n\n> We will roll out PointsUp so students earn badges for participation. It is fun and other schools use it.\n\n1. **Surface the competing values.** Name both sides honestly. Value A: visible, motivating participation. Value B: intrinsic engagement and equity, since public point rankings can shame slower or multilingual learners and reward speed over depth.\n2. **State the tension as a sentence.** \"We want to make participation visible (A) without converting the classroom into a ranking that penalizes students who need more processing time (B).\"\n3. **Set the test the tool must pass.** Any candidate must let participation be acknowledged privately or by effort, not only by a public leaderboard.\n4. **Decide and show the trade.** PointsUp's default is a public leaderboard with no private mode. Rejected as-is; the team will pilot it only if the leaderboard can be turned off, accepting slower setup in exchange for protecting Value B.\n\n**After (tension-first framing).** The revised proposal reads:\n\n> Tension: make participation visible without turning the room into a public ranking that penalizes slower or multilingual students. We are piloting PointsUp ONLY with the public leaderboard disabled and effort-based credit on. We rejected the default leaderboard because it trades equity for novelty. We will check after three weeks whether participation rose without widening the gap between fast and slow responders.\n\nThe decision is now defensible because the tension was named first and the tool was judged against it, with the trade-off made visible rather than hidden behind the word \"fun.\"\n\nThe whole point of this section is order of operations. Put the four moves of the tension-first process into the sequence that keeps the conflict governing the tool, not the reverse.\n\n<sequence-builder mode=\"order\" items='[\"Surface the two competing values and write both poles honestly\", \"State the tension as one sentence joining both poles\", \"Derive a pass/fail test the tool must meet FROM the tension\", \"Judge the candidate tool and show the trade you accepted\"]'></sequence-builder>\n\n## Decision Rule\n\nOrder the reasoning so the conflict governs the tool, not the reverse. Name the classroom value conflict first, weigh the technology affordance second, and confirm the policy boundary third. A tool choice is justified only when it protects students while improving a specific learning activity.\n\nBefore submitting the map, check that the trace answers three questions:\n\n1. **Purpose check:** What are the two values in tension, and which one is the tool quietly optimizing for by default?\n2. **Evidence check:** What pass/fail test, drawn from the tension, did the tool have to meet?\n3. **Revision check:** What trade did you accept, and what default did you reject to protect the second value?\n\nIf the tension has only one pole written down, you have a justification, not a negotiation.\n\n<self-explain prompt=\"Explain why deriving the tool's pass/fail test FROM the tension produces a different test than copying criteria off a vendor feature-comparison sheet.\" answer=\"The tension names the second pole, the value the default tool would quietly sacrifice, so a test derived from it asks for exactly the safeguard the brochure omits (for example, that participation can be credited privately or by effort, not only on a public leaderboard). A feature-comparison sheet lists what the vendor optimized for, which is the first pole. Deriving the test from the tension forces the tool to prove it protects the second value; copying the sheet only confirms the tool is good at the thing it was already built to do.\"></self-explain>\n\n## Common Misreadings\n\nThe recurring mistake is letting \"engagement\" or \"fun\" stand in for a value worth protecting. Engagement is real, but stated alone it names only one pole of the tension and hides the cost. A second misreading is treating a tension as a problem to be solved once; tensions are renegotiated every time the class composition changes, which is why the map records a trade rather than a fix. A third is mapping the tension after the tool is already chosen, which produces a rationalization rather than a decision.\n\n## Textbook Walkthrough\n\nApply the order-of-operations to a new case: a high-school department debating an AI writing-feedback tool. A tool-first team asks \"does it give good feedback?\" A tension-first team asks a harder question first.\n\nName both poles. Pole A: every student gets feedback within minutes instead of waiting a week. Pole B: feedback should not systematically misjudge students whose prose differs from the model's training register. Written as one sentence: \"We want fast feedback for all (A) without a tool that quietly scores multilingual writers lower (B).\"\n\nDerive the test from the tension, not the brochure. The tool must let the teacher see and override any score before students do, and must flag low-confidence judgments. Notice that this test would never appear on a feature comparison sheet; it comes from the second pole.\n\nDecide and show the trade. Adopt the tool for first-pass turnaround only, with teacher override on flagged essays, accepting slower throughput on those essays as the price of protecting Pole B. The walkthrough demonstrates the whole point: the tension, named first, generated a test the tool had to pass and a trade the department could defend.\n\n## What Strong Reading Looks Like\n\nAs you read, keep a two-column note: each **value** on the left, the **cost of maximizing it** on the right. If your right column is empty for a value, you have not found a tension yet, only a wish. For this topic specifically, watch for places where a single word (\"fun,\" \"rigor,\" \"efficiency\") is being used to hide a pole that should be stated.\n\nA strong reader of design tensions can answer four questions:\n\n1. What are the two legitimate values that pull against each other here?\n2. Which value does the tool optimize for by default if no one intervenes?\n3. What test, derived from the tension, would a candidate tool have to pass?\n4. What trade would change if the class gained more multilingual or slower-processing students?\n\n## Stronger Artifact Standard\n\nThe map is strong when both poles of the tension are written as a single honest sentence, a tool-passes test is derived from that sentence, and the accepted trade is stated with what was rejected. A map that lists only the chosen tool's benefits has skipped the tension entirely; mark that gap and ask BigAL to compare a one-pole map against a two-pole map rather than for a general explanation.\n\nA useful closing line sounds like: \"We rejected the default public leaderboard because it traded equity for novelty; we accepted slower setup to keep credit private.\" That names both the road not taken and the price paid.\n\n## Transfer Case\n\nMove the participation tension into a class that is almost entirely confident, fast-responding native speakers with no equity flags. The first pole (visible participation) is unchanged, but the second pole weakens: a public leaderboard may now cost little. The defensible call could flip toward enabling it. If your reasoning produces the same answer regardless of who is in the room, you mapped a slogan; if the trade shifts with the class, you understood the tension.\n\n## Artifact Studio\n\nOpen the studio packet: [download the Design Tension Studio map naming a classroom value conflict and negotiated decision packet](/downloads/summer2026/cat531-supplement/01-02-design-tensions-before-tool-choice-artifact.md).\n\n<artifact-studio artifact=\"Design Tension Studio map naming a classroom value conflict and negotiated decision\" course=\"CAT 531\" section=\"01.02\"></artifact-studio>\n\nUse the studio to create a before/after trace. The first version should show your starting interpretation. The revised version should show one meaningful change. The trace should explain why the change happened and why at least one possible suggestion was not accepted.\n\n## Adaptive Support\n\nALGET uses your annotations and artifact trace to choose support:\n\n- If your note is a **question**, ask for a concise explanation tied to **[TPACK](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Technological_pedagogical_content_knowledge)**.\n- If your note shows **confusion**, compare a weak and strong version of the Design Tension Studio map naming a classroom value conflict and negotiated decision.\n- If your note is an **insight**, transfer the same decision rule to a new audience or setting.\n- If your note makes a **connection**, show where the connection changes the artifact.\n\n<dynamic-scenario prompt=\"You are revising the Design Tension Studio map naming a classroom value conflict and negotiated decision for CAT 531 01.02. A peer comment points to an adaptive move issue connected to critical edtech studies. Choose the next revision move and the evidence that would show it helped.\"></dynamic-scenario>\n\n## Check Your Understanding\n\nBefore moving on, use the check to separate surface completion from a decision that another reader can inspect, question, and revise.\n\n<interactive-quiz question=\"Which clue should guide what support or revision comes next?\" options='[\"The section gives a longer explanation and a more polished visual.\", \"The AI tool generates the artifact without requiring learner revision.\", \"The learner completes the page and receives a generic score.\", \"The combination of the note, artifact evidence, confidence, and revision quality.\"]' correct-index=\"3\" conceptid=\"cat531_supplement_design_tensions_before_tool_choice\"></interactive-quiz>\n\n## Final Check\n\nBefore you move on, make sure your trace records evidence source, risk, support choice, and revision quality. That is what separates professional judgment from simple completion.\n\n## Concept Map\n\nTrace how naming a tension before the tool connects to evidence-based revision and the course anchors.\n\n<concept-map concepts='[\"cat531_supplement_foundations_of_cbi_and_design_tensions\", \"cat531_supplement_design_tensions_before_tool_choice\", \"evidence_based_revision\", \"ai_supported_learning\"]'></concept-map>\n\n## References\n\n- [TPACK](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Technological_pedagogical_content_knowledge). https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1467-9620.2006.00684.x\n- critical edtech studies. https://doi.org/10.4324/9781315670160\n- [AI and education datafication](https://doi.org/10.1080/17439884.2020.1686017)\n- teacher professional judgment. https://doi.org/10.1177/002205741319300304\n", "simulation": null, "illustration": null, "practice": {"problems": [{"id": "cat531_supplement_1_2_design_tensions_before_tool_choice_artifact_signal", "type": "multiple_choice", "concept_id": "cat531_supplement_design_tensions_before_tool_choice", "stem": "A department lead announces: 'We picked the new LMS analytics dashboard; now let's figure out what problems it solves.' Why does this sequence violate the principle of naming design tensions before tool choice?", "options": ["Analytics dashboards are never appropriate in classrooms.", "The tool was selected before the competing classroom values it must trade off were even surfaced, so the choice cannot be defended as a resolution of any tension.", "The department should have asked the vendor first.", "Dashboards must always be chosen by a committee."], "correct_index": 1, "explanation": "Naming tensions first means surfacing the competing values (for example, monitoring versus student autonomy) before adoption, so the tool is chosen to resolve a known tension rather than to manufacture one.", "misconception_id": "cat531_supplement_1_2_tool_first_then_rationalize", "difficulty": "easy", "concept_ids": ["cat531_supplement_foundations_of_cbi_and_design_tensions", "cat531_supplement_design_tensions_before_tool_choice", "evidence_based_revision", "ai_supported_learning"]}, {"id": "cat531_supplement_1_2_design_tensions_before_tool_choice_annotation_signal", "type": "multiple_choice", "concept_id": "cat531_supplement_design_tensions_before_tool_choice", "stem": "A teacher names the tension as 'fast feedback vs. teacher workload.' A colleague says this is not yet a design tension a tool choice can resolve. What is the most accurate critique?", "options": ["Both sides of a design tension should serve student interests; 'teacher workload' is a constraint, but the real value conflict is between fast feedback and the depth or fairness of that feedback.", "Feedback speed does not matter in any classroom.", "Tensions can only have one side, not two.", "Teacher workload should never be considered."], "correct_index": 0, "explanation": "A genuine design tension pits two values that both have legitimate claims on the learning. Workload is a real constraint, but framing the tension around competing learning values (speed vs. depth/fairness) is what a tool choice can actually be tested against.", "misconception_id": "cat531_supplement_1_2_constraint_mistaken_for_tension", "difficulty": "medium", "concept_ids": ["cat531_supplement_design_tensions_before_tool_choice", "evidence_based_revision"]}, {"id": "cat531_supplement_1_2_design_tensions_before_tool_choice_ai_boundary", "type": "multiple_choice", "concept_id": "cat531_supplement_ai_supported_learning", "stem": "Before choosing a tool, a teacher asks an AI to 'recommend the best app for differentiated reading.' The AI names one. What is the defensible way to use this output given the section's principle?", "options": ["Adopt the recommended app, since differentiation is a recognized goal.", "First use the AI to help enumerate the competing values at stake (for example, personalization vs. shared class discussion), then evaluate any tool against that named tension.", "Ask the AI to also write the lesson plan and grade it.", "Ignore the AI entirely because tool recommendations are off-limits."], "correct_index": 1, "explanation": "A tool recommendation skips the step that matters: surfacing the tension. AI is most defensibly used to help name competing values, after which any candidate tool is judged against that tension.", "misconception_id": "cat531_supplement_1_2_ai_recommendation_skips_tension", "difficulty": "medium", "concept_ids": ["ai_supported_learning", "cat531_supplement_design_tensions_before_tool_choice"]}, {"id": "cat531_supplement_1_2_design_tensions_before_tool_choice_calibration", "type": "multiple_choice", "concept_id": "cat531_supplement_design_tensions_before_tool_choice", "stem": "A teacher claims to have resolved the tension between 'monitoring engagement' and 'student privacy' by buying a camera-based attention tracker, saying 'the tension is gone now.' Why is this resolution not yet defensible?", "options": ["Cameras are always banned in schools.", "A defensible resolution shows how the chosen tool negotiates BOTH values and what was given up; declaring the tension gone hides the privacy cost that was actually paid.", "Engagement cannot be monitored at all.", "The teacher should have bought two trackers."], "correct_index": 1, "explanation": "Resolving a tension is not eliminating one side. A defensible decision shows the negotiated position, what each value gained, and what was traded away. Claiming the tension is 'gone' conceals the privacy cost.", "misconception_id": "cat531_supplement_1_2_resolution_means_eliminate_one_side", "difficulty": "hard", "concept_ids": ["cat531_supplement_design_tensions_before_tool_choice", "evidence_based_revision"]}]}, "content_version": {"content_version": "fd5ca4d91faad1c7c65f34bdcf76e2ac70eec368c08ad80c2c7c42a710748104", "algorithm": "sha256-mdx+meta+practice+misconceptions-v1", "parts": ["mdx", "meta", "practice", "misconceptions"]}, "misconceptions": [{"id": "cat531_supplement_1_2_tool_first_then_rationalize", "pattern": "tool_precedes_tension", "description": "Selecting a technology first and then searching for a problem it solves, instead of naming the value tension before the tool", "trigger": "learner adopts a tool and only afterward asks what classroom problem it addresses", "feedback": "Reverse the order. Surface the competing classroom values first, then choose a tool to negotiate that named tension.", "rail_action": "ask"}, {"id": "cat531_supplement_1_2_constraint_mistaken_for_tension", "pattern": "constraint_labeled_as_tension", "description": "Mislabeling a logistical constraint such as teacher workload as the design tension, instead of the competing learning values", "trigger": "learner frames the tension around convenience rather than two values that both have a claim on the learning", "feedback": "Restate the tension as two learning values in conflict (for example, feedback speed vs. feedback depth), keeping workload as a constraint, not the tension itself.", "rail_action": "represent"}, {"id": "cat531_supplement_1_2_ai_recommendation_skips_tension", "pattern": "ai_recommendation_replaces_analysis", "description": "Letting an AI tool recommendation stand in for the work of surfacing the value tension first", "trigger": "learner accepts an AI 'best tool' suggestion without naming the competing values it must negotiate", "feedback": "Use AI to help enumerate the competing values, then evaluate any candidate tool against that named tension.", "rail_action": "explain"}, {"id": "cat531_supplement_1_2_resolution_means_eliminate_one_side", "pattern": "resolution_as_elimination", "description": "Believing a design tension is resolved by eliminating one value rather than negotiating a defensible position between both", "trigger": "learner declares a tension 'gone' after adopting a tool, hiding the value that was traded away", "feedback": "Show the negotiated position: what each value gained, what was given up, and why the trade is defensible here.", "rail_action": "practice"}]}